Random Thoughts:
============================================================================================================
Service boundaries need to respect the following criteria:

-> handle transport layer failures

-> be request/response in nature to facilitate and encourage asynchronous communication

-> provide domain specific aggregation (caching / batching)
   --> aka dont hit the transport very often
   --> and if you do try to ship bulk data
   
   Transport layers are typically high latency high bandwidth ones. The best usage model is to
   aggregate data and send in bulk.
============================================================================================================
Services are different from components. Components are typically library aggregations and local by nature.
For example while building your local cache component if you need an allocator library dont make the allocator
a service. its cheap to link it as a local component. 

For local components we use OSGI's modularity layer continue to use that and stick with it. Don't use anything else
in OSGI.


Service Boundaries need to respect the conditions we described above so not every component boundary can be a 
service boundary.

N services can start off by being local on the same machine. For better load balancing you ll eventually need
to offload them to a separate machine to free up the pressure on your local CPU/MEMORY/IO subsystems.

When doing this offloading, think of the different services as a connected graph where the weight of the edges represent
the communication overhead between the services. You want to keep services that talk to each other either
    --> very frequently
    --> transfer lot of data 

 on the same machine. 
============================================================================================================
this concept can be extended to take a hierarchical form. The hierarchy being formed by the latency and distance between
two services closest being on the same machine. 
